# PR Description Guard - Cursor Rules

## Project Overview

PR Description Guard is a Chrome Extension (Manifest V3) that validates GitHub pull request descriptions to ensure they contain essential sections: "What changed", "Why", and "How it was tested". The extension provides non-intrusive, inline guidance without blocking PR creation or merging.

**Timeline:** 7-10 days MVP  
**Tech Stack:** TypeScript + Vite + Vitest  
**Target:** GitHub PR creation pages only

## Tech Stack Requirements

- **Language:** TypeScript (strict mode enabled)
- **Bundler:** Vite (fast builds, zero-config TypeScript)
- **Testing:** Vitest (TDD approach, >90% coverage target)
- **Styling:** Vanilla CSS with CSS Variables (no frameworks)
- **Extension:** Chrome Extension Manifest V3 only
- **No dependencies:** Pure JavaScript/TypeScript validation, no external libraries

## Project Structure

```
src/
  ├── content.ts      # Main orchestrator - initialization, SPA navigation, event binding
  ├── validator.ts    # Pure validation logic (NO DOM dependencies)
  ├── dom.ts          # DOM selectors and helpers with fallback chains
  └── styles.css      # Warning UI styles with CSS variables

tests/
  └── validator.test.ts  # Unit tests (TDD, >90% coverage)

icons/               # 16x16, 48x48, 128x128 PNG icons
manifest.json        # Manifest V3 configuration
vite.config.ts       # Vite build configuration
tsconfig.json        # TypeScript strict mode
package.json         # Dependencies and scripts
```

## File Responsibilities

### content.ts
- Handles initialization and cleanup
- Manages SPA navigation detection (URL checking on interval, Turbo events)
- Coordinates validation and UI updates
- Binds event listeners (input, paste)
- Retry mechanism: 3 attempts max, 500ms intervals

### validator.ts
- **PURE functions only** - NO DOM dependencies
- Exports `validatePRDescription(description: string): ValidationResult`
- Cleans template placeholders (HTML comments `<!-- comment -->` only)
- Uses precise regex patterns for section detection
- Returns structured validation results with rule IDs and messages

### dom.ts
- Selector functions with fallback chains
- `getDescriptionField(): HTMLTextAreaElement | null` - tries multiple selectors
- `getSubmitButton(): HTMLButtonElement | null` - JavaScript text matching
- Error handling for missing elements
- Logs warnings to console, never breaks GitHub

### styles.css
- CSS variables scoped to `.pr-guard-warning` element
- Dark mode support: `[data-color-mode="dark"]`, `.dark` class, `@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark)`
- Non-intrusive yellow/amber styling matching GitHub's design

## Validation Rules (Precise Specifications)

1. **EMPTY:** Description must contain non-whitespace content (after removing HTML comments)
2. **WHAT:** Must include "What changed" section
   - Pattern: `(?:^|\n)(?:#{1,3}|[\*_]{1,2})\s*what'?s?\s+changed[:\?]?`
   - Case-insensitive, handles apostrophes, punctuation
   - Does NOT match "Changes" alone (too generic)
3. **WHY:** Must include "Why" section
   - Pattern: `(?:^|\n)(?:#{1,3}|[\*_]{1,2})\s*why[:\?]?`
4. **TESTED:** Must include "How it was tested" section
   - Pattern: `(?:^|\n)(?:#{1,3}|[\*_]{1,2})\s*how\s+(?:it\s+)?was\s+tested[:\?]?`

## Code Style & Patterns

### TypeScript
- Use strict mode
- Define types explicitly: `ValidationResult`, `ValidationError`
- No `any` types
- Use ES modules (`export`, `import`)

### Error Handling
- **Always fail gracefully** - never break GitHub
- Silent failures with console warnings only
- No user-facing error messages
- Wrap initialization in try-catch

### DOM Selection
- Always use fallback selector chains
- Primary: `#pull_request_body`
- Fallbacks: `textarea[name="pull_request[body]"]`, `.comment-form-textarea`, `textarea[aria-label*="body"]`
- Log warnings if selectors fail
- Return `null` if element not found

### SPA Navigation
- **DO NOT intercept** `history.pushState` or `history.replaceState` (too fragile)
- Use URL pathname checking on interval (2-3 seconds)
- Listen to `popstate` event (browser navigation)
- Optional: Listen to Turbo `turbo:load` event
- Fallback: Lightweight MutationObserver (direct children only)

### Performance
- Debounce validation: 300ms delay
- Lightweight observers (not entire subtree)
- Minimal DOM queries
- Store handler references for cleanup

### Memory Management
- Always remove event listeners on cleanup
- Clear timers on navigation
- Use data attributes to track initialization
- Prevent duplicate initializations

## Testing Requirements

### TDD Approach
- Write tests FIRST, then implement
- Target >90% coverage for validation logic
- Test all edge cases: empty, whitespace, templates, variations, punctuation

### Test Cases (validator.test.ts)
- Empty string → fails EMPTY
- Whitespace only → fails EMPTY
- Missing sections → fails WHAT, WHY, TESTED
- Valid sections → passes all rules
- Case insensitivity
- Apostrophes ("What's changed")
- Punctuation (colons, question marks)
- Template placeholders (HTML comments ignored)
- "Changes" alone does NOT pass WHAT

### Integration Testing
- Test on real GitHub (not local)
- Test with PR templates
- Test with different PR types (new, edit, draft)
- Test with multiple Chrome versions
- Test screen readers (NVDA, JAWS, VoiceOver)

## Design Principles

1. **Non-intrusive** - Guide, don't block
2. **Fail gracefully** - Never break GitHub (silent failure, console warnings)
3. **Performance first** - Minimal overhead (debouncing, lightweight observers)
4. **Accessibility** - Proper ARIA roles (`role="alert"` first, then `role="status"`), `aria-live="polite"`)
5. **Maintainable** - Clean, testable code (>90% coverage)
6. **User-friendly** - Clear, helpful messaging

## Constraints & Scope

### In Scope (MVP)
- ✅ Validation of PR descriptions on GitHub
- ✅ Inline warning display (simple, non-collapsible)
- ✅ Real-time validation (debounced 300ms)
- ✅ Template placeholder handling (HTML comments only)
- ✅ Dark mode support
- ✅ SPA navigation handling
- ✅ Graceful error handling
- ✅ Integration testing on real GitHub

### Out of Scope (Post-MVP)
- ❌ AI or auto-writing
- ❌ Backend services or analytics
- ❌ GitHub API integration
- ❌ Posting comments on PRs
- ❌ Blocking PR creation or merging
- ❌ Custom validation rules (user-defined)
- ❌ Settings UI or popup
- ❌ Collapsible warnings
- ❌ GitLab or Bitbucket support
- ❌ Enterprise GitHub support

### Technical Constraints
- Manifest V3 only (no V2)
- Content script only (no background service worker)
- DOM-based only (no GitHub API calls)
- Chrome only (no Firefox/Edge in MVP)
- GitHub.com only (no enterprise GitHub)
- No permissions required

## Implementation Guidelines

### When Writing Code

1. **Follow TDD** - Write tests first for validator.ts
2. **Keep validator.ts pure** - No DOM, no side effects
3. **Use fallback selectors** - Always have multiple selector options
4. **Debounce everything** - Input events, initialization
5. **Clean up properly** - Remove listeners, clear timers on navigation
6. **Use CSS variables** - For theming and dark mode
7. **Namespace CSS classes** - Use `pr-guard-` prefix
8. **Log to console** - For debugging, never show user errors
9. **Handle edge cases** - Paste events, multiple tabs, AJAX loading, markdown preview

### When Writing Tests

1. **Test edge cases** - Empty, whitespace, templates, variations
2. **Test regex patterns** - All markdown formats, case variations, punctuation
3. **Test error messages** - User-friendly, actionable
4. **Achieve >90% coverage** - Especially for validation logic

### When Writing CSS

1. **Use CSS variables** - Scoped to `.pr-guard-warning`
2. **Support dark mode** - GitHub classes + media query fallback
3. **Match GitHub's design** - Non-intrusive yellow/amber styling
4. **Namespace everything** - `pr-guard-` prefix for all classes

### When Handling Errors

1. **Silent failure** - Log to console, never show user
2. **Never break GitHub** - Extension should degrade gracefully
3. **Structured logging** - Include context in console warnings
4. **Retry mechanisms** - 3 attempts max, 500ms intervals

## Common Patterns

### Debounce Utility
```typescript
function debounce(func: Function, wait: number): Function
// Returns debounced version, clears timeout on new calls
```

### Retry Pattern
```typescript
// Retry up to 3 times, 500ms intervals
// Log warning if all retries fail
// Exit silently, don't break GitHub
```

### Selector Fallback
```typescript
// Try selectors in order
// Return first match or null
// Log warning if all fail
```

### Cleanup Pattern
```typescript
// Remove event listeners
// Clear timers
// Remove DOM elements
// Reset initialization markers
```

## Quality Checklist

Before considering code complete:
- [ ] TypeScript strict mode passes
- [ ] Unit tests pass (>90% coverage)
- [ ] No console errors in normal operation
- [ ] Handles all edge cases
- [ ] Proper cleanup on navigation
- [ ] Accessibility attributes set correctly
- [ ] Dark mode works
- [ ] No memory leaks
- [ ] Fails gracefully if GitHub DOM changes

## Chrome Web Store Compliance

### Required Compliance Rules

**ALWAYS comply with Chrome Web Store policies. Violations result in rejection or removal.**

#### Privacy Policy Requirements
- **MUST have privacy policy** - Required for all extensions
- Privacy policy URL must be accessible and accurate
- Must clearly state what data is collected (if any)
- For this extension: State that NO data is collected, stored, or transmitted
- Privacy policy must be linked in manifest.json and Web Store listing

#### Data Collection Restrictions
- **NO data collection** - Extension does not collect, store, or transmit user data
- **NO analytics** - Do not use Google Analytics, Mixpanel, or any tracking
- **NO external API calls** - All validation happens locally
- **NO user identification** - Do not track users or their PRs
- **NO cookies or storage** - Do not use localStorage, sessionStorage, or cookies
- **NO network requests** - Do not make HTTP/HTTPS requests to any server

#### Manifest V3 Requirements
- **MUST use Manifest V3** - Manifest V2 is deprecated
- **No background service worker** - Content script only for MVP
- **No permissions** - Extension runs without special permissions
- **Proper content script matches** - Only match GitHub PR pages
- **Icons required** - 16x16, 48x48, 128x128 PNG files
- **Version format** - Use semantic versioning (e.g., "1.0.0")

#### Content Policies
- **No deceptive practices** - Extension does what it claims
- **No malware or harmful code** - Code must be safe and secure
- **No unauthorized access** - Only access GitHub PR pages as intended
- **No user data misuse** - No data to misuse (extension doesn't collect data)
- **Respect user privacy** - All processing is local, no data leaves browser

#### User Data Handling
- **Zero data collection** - Extension never collects user data
- **Local processing only** - All validation happens in browser
- **No data transmission** - No data sent to servers
- **No data storage** - No persistent storage of any kind
- **No user tracking** - No tracking of user behavior or PRs

#### Permissions Best Practices
- **Minimal permissions** - Request only what's absolutely necessary
- **No host permissions** - Extension doesn't need host permissions
- **No activeTab permission** - Not needed for content script
- **No storage permission** - Not needed (no data storage)
- **No background permission** - Not needed (no background script)

#### Web Store Listing Requirements
- **Accurate description** - Describe exactly what extension does
- **Screenshots required** - 1280x800 or 640x400 pixels
- **Privacy policy URL** - Must be publicly accessible
- **Support information** - Provide support contact if needed
- **Clear purpose** - Users must understand extension's function

### Privacy Policy Content

The privacy policy MUST state:
```
PR Description Guard does not collect, store, or transmit any user data.

All validation happens locally in your browser.
No data is sent to any server.
No analytics are used.
No cookies are set.
No user information is tracked.
```

### Compliance Checklist

Before submitting to Chrome Web Store:
- [ ] Manifest V3 compliant
- [ ] Privacy policy created and accessible
- [ ] Privacy policy URL added to manifest.json
- [ ] No data collection code (verify no network requests)
- [ ] No analytics code (verify no tracking)
- [ ] No storage usage (verify no localStorage/cookies)
- [ ] Icons provided (16x16, 48x48, 128x128)
- [ ] Screenshots prepared (1280x800 or 640x400)
- [ ] Accurate description in listing
- [ ] All permissions justified (should be none for MVP)
- [ ] Code review for compliance violations
- [ ] Test extension doesn't make network requests
- [ ] Verify extension works without internet connection

### Code Review for Compliance

**When reviewing code, check:**
1. **No fetch/XMLHttpRequest** - Verify no network requests
2. **No localStorage/sessionStorage** - Verify no data storage
3. **No cookies** - Verify no cookie usage
4. **No external scripts** - Verify no third-party scripts loaded
5. **No analytics** - Verify no tracking code
6. **No user identification** - Verify no user tracking
7. **Privacy policy accurate** - Verify policy matches actual behavior

### Violation Consequences

**Chrome Web Store violations result in:**
- Extension rejection during review
- Extension removal from store
- Developer account suspension (for repeated violations)
- Legal issues (for privacy violations)

**ALWAYS verify compliance before submission.**

## Notes

- Timeline is 7-10 days for MVP
- Setup time (TypeScript, Vite, Vitest) is ~1-2 hours one-time cost
- Test coverage >90% is achievable with TDD approach
- Keep it simple - MVP is minimal, no feature creep
- Always prioritize: non-intrusive, fail gracefully, performance
- **ALWAYS comply with Chrome Web Store policies** - This is non-negotiable
